# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Restated from apps/mobile/.gitignore — CREDENTIALS. Do not weaken.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# apps/mobile/AuthKey_*.p8 is written by the "Create Apple API Key File" step in every mobile
# workflow, before `eas build`. `eas submit` reads it on the GitHub runner, NOT on the Expo
# builder, so the builder never needs it and it must not be uploaded.
*.p8
*.p12
*.key
*.jks
*.mobileprovision
*.keystore
!debug.keystore
.env
.env.*.local
!.env.template

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Restated from apps/mobile/.gitignore — native/build artifacts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# apps/mobile/ios and apps/mobile/android have ZERO tracked files: this is the managed Expo
# workflow and `expo prebuild` regenerates both on the builder. Shipping local copies only
# risks a stale native project.
.expo/
web-build/
*.jsbundle
*.ipa
*.hmap
*.xcuserstate
xcuserdata
DerivedData
/apps/mobile/ios/Pods/
.gradle
local.properties
*.iml
*.hprof
.cxx/
.metro-health-check*
**/fastlane/report.xml
**/fastlane/Preview.html
**/fastlane/screenshots
**/fastlane/test_output

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Workspaces the mobile app does not need
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EAS uploads the repo ROOT (apps/mobile is a yarn workspace) and runs
#
#     yarn install --frozen-lockfile --production false
#
# in that root, so it installs the dependencies of EVERY workspace in the archive, not just the
# mobile app's. A workspace whose package.json is not in the archive is not a workspace on the
# builder, so its dependencies are never resolved, fetched, linked or built. This list is the
# only lever that stops that.
#
# THE FAILURE THIS FIXES — every iOS build errored from 2025-11-13 to 2026-08-16. The GitHub log
# only ever said "Unknown error, see the Install dependencies build phase"; Expo's own log said:
#
#     error .../node_modules/@storybook/nextjs/node_modules/sharp,
#           .../node_modules/plasmo/node_modules/sharp: Command failed.
#     Command: node install/check
#     sharp: Detected globally-installed libvips v8.16.1
#     sharp: Attempting to build from source via node-gyp
#     ../metadata.cc:237:46: error: no member named 'NewOrCopy' in 'Napi::Buffer<char>'   (x5)
#     make: *** [Release/obj.target/sharp-darwin-arm64/metadata.o] Error 1
#
#   1. apps/extensions                                   -> plasmo@0.90.5            -> sharp 0.33.5
#      packages/toolkit/examples/next-boilerplate-ixartz -> @storybook/nextjs@8.6.18  -> sharp 0.33.5
#      Both land NESTED, because the repo root already hoists sharp 0.34.5.
#
#   2. The EAS macOS image ships libvips 8.16.1 GLOBALLY. sharp 0.33.5 declares
#      `config.libvips: ">=8.15.3"` and its install/check.js does
#          if (useGlobalLibvips()) buildFromSource(...)
#      so it DISCARDS its own prebuilt @img/sharp-darwin-arm64 binary and compiles from source.
#      Root sharp 0.34.5 escapes only because it wants libvips >=8.17.3 — one minor release of
#      margin, controlled by Expo, not by us.
#
#   3. sharp 0.33.5 lists node-addon-api only as a devDependency, so nothing installs a copy for
#      it and its build walks up to the ROOT-hoisted one. apps/extensions also pulls
#      plasmo -> @parcel/core -> lmdb -> node-addon-api ^4.3.0, and that 4.3.0 is what yarn 1
#      hoists. `Napi::Buffer<char>::NewOrCopy` does not exist before node-addon-api 6.x
#      (verified: absent in 1.7.2 and 4.3.0; present in 6.1.0, 7.1.1 and 8.5.0).
#
# One workspace supplied the sharp that compiles; the other supplied the node-addon-api that
# cannot compile it. Excluding them removes both halves at once.
#
# WHY THE LIST IS THIS BROAD — excluding only those two would fix today's failure and leave the
# trap armed: any workspace here may add a native or install-scripted dependency at any time and
# break iOS again, visibly only in Expo's log. apps/mobile declares NO @ever-teams/* dependency
# and has no source coupling outside itself, so the required set is provably tiny:
#
#     apps/mobile              the app being built
#     packages/types           the ROOT package.json devDepends on "@ever-teams/types": "*"
#     packages/eslint-config   packages/types devDepends on "@ever-teams/eslint-config": "*"
#
# Those three must never be excluded: yarn.lock has no @ever-teams/* entries, so they resolve
# only by workspace linking, and excluding one turns a link into a registry lookup that fails
# --frozen-lockfile at [2/5].
#
# This is a GATE, not a removal. Every workspace stays in the repo, in git, and in every other
# build; only the mobile upload archive skips them.
#
# Verified before shipping on the builder's exact toolchain (yarn 1.22.22, node 24.14.0): with
# these 25 workspaces removed, `yarn install --frozen-lockfile --production false` completes in
# 438s and exits 0. --frozen-lockfile fails on lockfile entries that are MISSING, never on ones
# merely unused, so the extra yarn.lock entries are harmless. The resulting tree hoists
# node-addon-api 6.1.0, leaving react-native-bootsplash's sharp 0.32.6 as the only source build,
# which compiles cleanly against 6.x.
#
# Defence in depth: apps/mobile/eas.json sets SHARP_IGNORE_GLOBAL_LIBVIPS=1 on every build
# profile. All three sharp versions in this tree honour it and it short-circuits before any
# pkg-config probe, so no sharp consults the builder's global libvips at all. Keep BOTH — this
# list stops the dependencies arriving; that flag stops any sharp that does arrive from
# compiling, and unlike a version pin it does not expire when Expo bumps libvips.
#
# Before adding a workspace back, check what install scripts it drags onto a macOS builder.
apps/chatgpt
apps/extensions
apps/playground
apps/server-web
apps/storybook
apps/web
packages/api
packages/config-tailwind
packages/constants
packages/hooks
packages/services
packages/toolkit
packages/toolkit-types
packages/toolkit-ui
packages/ts-config
packages/ui
packages/utils
